<h4 id="appstorage--userdefault-property-wrapper">@AppStorage — UserDefault Property Wrapper</h4>

<p>This <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@AppStorage</code> is SwiftUI’s wrapper for UserDefault, used for local storage similar to NSUserDefault — storing key and value or object, ultimately archived to a plist file.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<div class="language-swift highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre><span class="kd">@AppStorage</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"shouldUseDarkMode"</span><span class="p">)</span> <span class="kd">private</span> <span class="k">var</span> <span class="nv">shouldUseDarkMode</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="kt">Bool</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="kc">false</span>
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div></div>

<p>When you declare a variable called <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">shouldUseDarkMode</code> in SwiftUI with a default value of false, when UserDefault retrieves a value, this <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">shouldUseDarkMode</code> member variable will be the retrieved real value. If nothing is retrieved, false serves as the fallback default.</p>

<p>@AppStorage(“shouldUseDarkMode”) — the string here is the key used when retrieving the value from UserDefault.</p>

